    "question": {
      ":question/id": "08c38953-f704-56cc-b3e1-73f07fd4a27c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny\\prime=\\frac{5 y}{x},\\qquad x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=- 4 x^{5}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f\\prime(x)=- 16 x^{4}.$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and $y\\prime=f\\prime(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement.\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=- 4 x^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\prime(x)&=\\left(- 4 x^{5}\\right)\\prime \\\\ &=-4\\cdot 5 x^{4} \\\\ &=- 20 x^{4} \\\\ &\\not\\equiv - 16 x^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=- 4 x^{5}$ and $y\\prime=- 20 x^{4}$ into the original equation gives an identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\prime &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5 y}{x} \\\\ - 20 x^{4} &\\overset{?}{=}\\frac{5\\left(- 4 x^{5}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 20 x^{4} &=- 20 x^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=- 4 x^{5}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
